  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 249 AM CST Fri Nov 6 2026\n\nThrough mid to late next week (Thursday)...\n\nThis morning into this weekend: GOES East water vapor and RAP\nanalysis indicate cold core upper low over the Great Lakes and\nlagging trough axis to the southwest and another over the Desert \nSouthwest. At the surface, a reinforcing cold front is situated to\nnorthwest in the ArkLaTex into Mid South with strong 1025mb high \npressure in the northern Plains. This pattern will continue to \nfilter in cooler, drier air in the wake. Highs will continue to be\nseasonably warm today (68F to 80F today), with much more \nseasonable and cooler lows (38F to 52F). REFS dense fog probs \nremain northwest of the Natchez Trace Parkway. Some areas there\nis crossover temps very favorable for fog (5F to 10F). Based on \nthat, inherited Limited HWO graphic for dense fog seems on track.\n\nAs the upper trough swings east today, with subtle shortwave \nridging move in this weekend. This will be in advance of an upper\nlow over Baja California into west-central Mexico, with increased\nisentropic ascent and clouds into the weekend. 1020-1025mb \nsurface high will build in late weekend into the Mid South, \nbringing much cooler temperatures both Saturday and Sunday (low \nto upper 60s north of Interstate 20, while upper 60s to low 70s \nto the south of Interstate 20). Seasonably cool lows, some 5F to \n12F below, will be the norm (38F to 50F Friday night and 33F to \n42F Saturday and Sunday nights). Patchy frost remains a concern \nacross the region Saturday and Sunday nights. There will be some \ngradient wind this weekend into early next week, but remain light\nenough to support some frost potential, especially in low-lying \nand sheltered areas. Ensure plants, pets, and people are considered\nwith more routine lows nearing frost to light freeze potential. \nKept inherited Limited HWO graphic for patchy frost during these \nnights, mainly in north-central, east to northeast MS.\n\nEarly to mid next week: Quiet, cloudy and mostly dry pattern will\npersist into the following week. Eastward ejection of upper low \nis slower than yesterday morning, with the center over west TX. \nThis will keep moist, isentropic ascent and cloudy conditions\ndraped across the Gulf Coast states through at least mid week \n(Wednesday). Cannot rule out an isolated rain shower during this \ntime but with upper low filling in and becoming more open wave,\nascent will be less as it propagates eastward. With some thermal \ntemps warming, highs will moderate from seasonable early week to \nseasonably warm mid week (68F to 76F Monday to 70F to 78F by \nWednesday). Lows will remain seasonably cool but gradually less \ncold. The synoptic pattern becomes less confident in late week:\nsynoptic amplitude becomes less confident in western zone of jet\nenergy across the Plains from the GFS while the Euro shows more\ndeep mean troughing for areas east of the Plains. Cluster analysis\nindicate a good percentage of members from the ECENS and GEFS (30\nto 45 percent) favor a clusters/pattern closer to the Euro, GEFS\nand AI (global model suite and ensemble). This favors a drier and\ncooler pattern into late week. Will have to monitor the synoptic\nsetup as a potential target of opportunity. /DC/",
